#
# Files and directories excluded from the packaged, ready-to-download releases of this
# site template. The format of this file is documented at
# https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes.
#
# NOTE: `export-ignore` only affects the packaged tarball. Everything below stays fully
# available to anyone who clones the repository from git — which is deliberate, see
# README.md, "Development process".
#

# --- Starter kit defaults -------------------------------------------------------------
# A list of words that are legitimate, but not recognized by the spell checker used in CI.
/.cspell-project-words.txt export-ignore

# CI configuration and tests; useful for development, but irrelevant to end users.
/.github export-ignore
/.gitlab-ci.yml export-ignore
/tests export-ignore

# Tugboat configuration is used to generate live previews of the site template, but isn't needed
# by end users.
/.tugboat export-ignore

# --- Development tooling --------------------------------------------------------------
# Linter configuration for this repository; not part of the delivered template.
/.eslintrc.json export-ignore

# --- Ágora process layer (D-015.2) ----------------------------------------------------
# The methodology behind this template: instructions for the assistants used to build it,
# and the signed decision record. Public in the repository as disclosure of method, but it
# does not belong inside a release the end user installs.
/CLAUDE.md export-ignore
/.claude export-ignore
/specs export-ignore

# --- Deliberately NOT export-ignored (D-015.1) ----------------------------------------
# AGENTS.md is product, not process: it ships with the template and guides AI assistants
# working on a site built WITH Ágora. Do not add it to this file.

# --- Line endings (checkout attribute, not export-ignore) ------------------------------
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set at system scope by the Git for Windows installer, so a
# fresh clone checks these shell scripts out as CRLF on any default Windows machine,
# breaking their shebang. `eol` governs checkout in every clone, unlike `export-ignore`
# above, which only shapes the packaged tarball. Scoped to shell scripts, never a
# blanket `* text=auto`.
# ONE LINE ENDING EVERYWHERE, ADDED 2026-08-25, AND IT IS A DEFECT FIX RATHER THAN A
# PREFERENCE. The line below used to be scoped to tests/bin and *.sh only, on the
# reasoning that a CRLF shebang does not execute. That was right and insufficient.
#
# What T-802's container measured: git's BLOBS are LF (1 genuine CRLF blob out of 179),
# but with core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout materialises 50 of them as CRLF - and
# MSYS grep CANNOT SEE those carriage returns. a grep for a carriage return in `recipe.yml` returns 0 while
# `od -c` shows the carriage return. So end-anchored checks PASS ON THIS HOST BY ACCIDENT:
# run the same
# runners in a Linux container against the same working tree and two of them fail, because
# GNU grep 3.11 sees what MSYS grep hides.
#
# CI and end users were never affected - they read the LF blobs. The host was the liar,
# and it was lying in the direction that says everything is fine.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes every working tree LF, on Windows and macOS alike, so the host,
# the container, CI and the packaged tarball all read the same bytes. [andres] confirmed
# on 2026-08-25 that he will work from macOS too, which turns this from tidiness into a
# requirement: a project whose checks depend on the shell cannot afford three dialects of
# the same file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Kept explicit even though the line above now covers them: a CRLF shebang does not
# execute, and that failure is silent on the runner and invisible here.
tests/bin/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
